  "output": {
    "statement": "For every rational t outside {0,1,-1}, the third coordinate c of dkp_square_family(t) is not an integer.",
    "assumptions": [
      "The six coordinates are given by the exact formulas in the supplied dkp_square_family definition.",
      "t is rational and t is not 0, 1, or -1."
    ],
    "quantifiers": [
      "For every rational t not in {0,1,-1}, with no height bound."
    ],
    "objective": "proof",
    "claim_scope": "partial",
    "proof": "Write t=m/n in lowest terms with n>0. The domain restriction gives m≠0 and m²≠n². Set H=m²+n² and D=m²−n². Substituting t=m/n into c=8(t²−1)³/(9(t²+1)³) gives c=8D³/(9H³). Since m and n are nonzero, H>0 and 0<|D|<H: indeed, for positive m²,n², the absolute difference is strictly less than their sum. Consequently 0<|c|=(8/9)(|D|/H)³<8/9<1. A nonzero integer cannot have absolute value less than 1, so c is not an integer.",
    "dependency_ids": [],
    "gaps": [
      "This proves nonintegrality only for c. It does not prove that a, b, e, f, and g are nonintegers for every allowed rational t, nor does it refute the six-coordinate claim with a counterexample.",
      "The suggested valuation, divisibility, resultant, and exceptional-factor arguments for the other coordinates remain unproved; no resultant certificate is used here.",
      "This auxiliary family result does not resolve either the unrestricted-sign or all-positive original six-element existence question."
    ],
